  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.5 z²) / 2, drawn at half its value as a height. x (left-right) is the steep direction: plain steps there scale by 1 − 2.4 × rate, so swings grow for rates above 0.83.",
    "Noise is added to both slope components with spread sqrt((64 − b) / (63 b)), as when sampling b of 64 points: largest at batch 1, exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The purple arrow is the next step, −rate × noisy slope; the dark arrow is −rate × true slope. Both are drawn 4 times their real length (one scale); a zero step is hidden and its length reads 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 4.8 or the ball leaves the bowl (diverged). Off-bowl positions are drawn at the rim; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 steps; the kept path is the same. New run continues the noise sequence; Reset replays it from the start with the fixed seed.",
    "Running takes 5 steps per second at speed 1."
  ]
}